{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And Moses did not know that the skin of his face was qaran [= was horned/radiant] when He spoke with him.</b> The meaning is: when HaShem spoke with him. And this is because the glory appeared upon his face; therefore He warned him, like the warning of the firmament." "<b>And the word qeren [= horn].</b> From the derivative of קרנים מידו לו (Habakkuk 3:4), for thus is written at the beginning of the verse, ונגה כאור תהיה (ibid.), and behold it says after it קרנים מידו לו — the meaning will be doubled as is the rule. The bones of the villain Hav[i] ought to be ground, who said that because he did not eat bread, the face of Moses dried up like horn. <b>And the meaning of wayyira'u [= they were afraid].</b> Because his face was ugly. And how did this accursed one not open his eyes, for a person is not afraid to approach another person except on account of a wondrous thing newly appearing in him such as he has never seen the like. And there is no person who has not seen the face of a dead man, yet he is not afraid to approach him. And furthermore, if this were so, why was the veil not always upon his face, and why did he always remove it when speaking with Israel? Did he not see that when he was a hundred and twenty years old his eye was not dim nor his moisture fled, and even more so when he was eighty?"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {1 "The identity of 'Hav[i]' (חוי) is uncertain; it may be an epithet for a specific medieval critic. The phrase 'his moisture fled' renders לא נס לחה literally; the idiom appears in Deuteronomy 34:7."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:40:31.884711660Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}